## What the concurrency cost

Three concurrent Playwright suites drove load to 17 on 8 cores. Measured consequences:
- C1's first full run was invalidated (a killed Playwright survived `pkill` and raced its
  replacement on the same port and database — two summary blocks under one header). Discarded
  and re-run, not quoted.
- Both resume cards found the shared `hq_test_go` / `hq_test_e2e` databases at a goose version
  the other card had set, and `hq_test_e2e` refused to boot. Each worked around it with its own
  databases rather than dropping databases it did not create (B-16).
- Every G6 reviewer and fix round was given a distinct database prefix and TEST_PORT by the
  orchestrator for this reason.
